WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.307
<v Instructor>So now let's talk about VPC Flow Logs.</v>

2
00:00:02.307 --> 00:00:04.590
VPC Flow Logs allow you to capture information

3
00:00:04.590 --> 00:00:07.050
from IP traffic going into your interfaces.

4
00:00:07.050 --> 00:00:10.110
So that could be at the VPC level, the subnet level,

5
00:00:10.110 --> 00:00:13.050
or the elastic network interface, ENI level.

6
00:00:13.050 --> 00:00:15.390
So you have three kinds of flow logs.

7
00:00:15.390 --> 00:00:16.860
They're very helpful to monitor

8
00:00:16.860 --> 00:00:18.840
and troubleshoot connectivity issues

9
00:00:18.840 --> 00:00:20.460
happening within your VPC.

10
00:00:20.460 --> 00:00:22.680
So these flow logs can be sent to Amazon S3,

11
00:00:22.680 --> 00:00:25.470
CloudWatch Logs, and Kinesis Data Firehose.

12
00:00:25.470 --> 00:00:26.850
And they will capture information

13
00:00:26.850 --> 00:00:31.500
for also AWS managed interfaces such as ELB, RDS,

14
00:00:31.500 --> 00:00:34.500
ElastiCache, Redshift, WorkSpaces, your NAT gateway,

15
00:00:34.500 --> 00:00:36.450
your transit gateway, and so on.

16
00:00:36.450 --> 00:00:38.280
So in this graph, well, it's very simple.

17
00:00:38.280 --> 00:00:40.290
Your flow logs are at the VPC level, for example,

18
00:00:40.290 --> 00:00:41.820
or subnet or ENI level

19
00:00:41.820 --> 00:00:44.640
and they're sent to CloudWatch and Amazon S3.

20
00:00:44.640 --> 00:00:47.730
So this is what a VPC Flow Log looks like.

21
00:00:47.730 --> 00:00:49.920
So there is a format associated with it

22
00:00:49.920 --> 00:00:52.860
but there is a version, account-id, interface-id,

23
00:00:52.860 --> 00:00:55.260
source address, destination address, source port,

24
00:00:55.260 --> 00:00:57.540
destination port, protocol, packets, bytes, start,

25
00:00:57.540 --> 00:01:00.330
and action, and log-status.

26
00:01:00.330 --> 00:01:02.460
So this is metadata about the network packets

27
00:01:02.460 --> 00:01:05.160
going into your VPC.

28
00:01:05.160 --> 00:01:07.650
And you don't need to remember this at a high level, right?

29
00:01:07.650 --> 00:01:10.080
But let's have a look at what information

30
00:01:10.080 --> 00:01:11.760
we can get out of these flow logs.

31
00:01:11.760 --> 00:01:13.470
So the source address and the destination address

32
00:01:13.470 --> 00:01:15.780
help identify problematic IP.

33
00:01:15.780 --> 00:01:18.540
This is where you see if an IP is repeatedly being denied,

34
00:01:18.540 --> 00:01:19.830
maybe there is something wrong with that IP

35
00:01:19.830 --> 00:01:22.380
or maybe you're being attacked by a specific IP.

36
00:01:22.380 --> 00:01:24.870
Srcport and dstport helps you identify

37
00:01:24.870 --> 00:01:26.130
the problematic ports.

38
00:01:26.130 --> 00:01:29.430
Action is going to be either accept or reject,

39
00:01:29.430 --> 00:01:31.860
and so it's going to say whether or not it's a success

40
00:01:31.860 --> 00:01:34.530
or failure at the SG or NACL level,

41
00:01:34.530 --> 00:01:35.970
and we'll see this in the very next slide.

42
00:01:35.970 --> 00:01:38.100
And so this can be used, the VPC Flow Logs

43
00:01:38.100 --> 00:01:40.260
to do analytics on usage patterns

44
00:01:40.260 --> 00:01:43.920
or detect malicious behavior, port scans, and so on.

45
00:01:43.920 --> 00:01:44.910
Now, to query these flow logs,

46
00:01:44.910 --> 00:01:46.290
you have two ways of doing it.

47
00:01:46.290 --> 00:01:48.600
The best way is to do Athena on S3,

48
00:01:48.600 --> 00:01:50.670
or if you wanted to do a streaming analysis

49
00:01:50.670 --> 00:01:52.680
you could use CloudWatch Logs Insights.

50
00:01:52.680 --> 00:01:54.810
So here are some examples that you can have a look

51
00:01:54.810 --> 00:01:56.610
on your own time for Flow Logs.

52
00:01:56.610 --> 00:01:59.340
So how do we use Flow Logs to troubleshoot security group

53
00:01:59.340 --> 00:02:00.840
and NACL issues?

54
00:02:00.840 --> 00:02:02.190
Well, we look at the ACTION field.

55
00:02:02.190 --> 00:02:05.220
So let's have a look at the typical incoming request

56
00:02:05.220 --> 00:02:06.330
for your NACL and your subnet.

57
00:02:06.330 --> 00:02:08.250
So remember, your NACLs are stateless

58
00:02:08.250 --> 00:02:10.800
and your security groups are stateful.

59
00:02:10.800 --> 00:02:11.910
So what happens?

60
00:02:11.910 --> 00:02:13.740
Well, if we get an inbound reject,

61
00:02:13.740 --> 00:02:16.110
so we see that the inbound request

62
00:02:16.110 --> 00:02:19.350
coming from the outside to our EC2 instance is rejected.

63
00:02:19.350 --> 00:02:21.390
That can mean that either from this graph

64
00:02:21.390 --> 00:02:23.670
the NACL is refusing the request,

65
00:02:23.670 --> 00:02:26.550
or the security group is refusing the request.

66
00:02:26.550 --> 00:02:28.110
And this makes sense, right?

67
00:02:28.110 --> 00:02:32.040
But if we have a inbound accept and an outbound reject,

68
00:02:32.040 --> 00:02:34.170
it can only mean a NACL issue.

69
00:02:34.170 --> 00:02:35.003
Why?

70
00:02:35.003 --> 00:02:36.690
Well, because your security group is stateful,

71
00:02:36.690 --> 00:02:39.510
and if the inbound is allowed because of the accept,

72
00:02:39.510 --> 00:02:41.880
automatically, the outbound will be allowed

73
00:02:41.880 --> 00:02:44.730
thanks to the statefulness of your security group.

74
00:02:44.730 --> 00:02:46.920
So after outgoing requests, similar analysis, right?

75
00:02:46.920 --> 00:02:48.570
This is the diagram we know already.

76
00:02:48.570 --> 00:02:50.700
And so if you get an outbound reject,

77
00:02:50.700 --> 00:02:53.280
then you have a NACL or a security group issue.

78
00:02:53.280 --> 00:02:55.890
But if you get an outbound accept and an inbound reject,

79
00:02:55.890 --> 00:02:58.530
then it must mean a NACL issue.

80
00:02:58.530 --> 00:03:00.600
So let's have a look at a few architectures

81
00:03:00.600 --> 00:03:02.160
for your VPC Flow Logs.

82
00:03:02.160 --> 00:03:04.860
So they can flow into CloudWatch Logs, as we know.

83
00:03:04.860 --> 00:03:06.090
And then we can use something called

84
00:03:06.090 --> 00:03:07.830
CloudWatch Contributor Insights,

85
00:03:07.830 --> 00:03:10.620
for example, to find the top 10 IP addresses

86
00:03:10.620 --> 00:03:12.900
that contribute to the most amount of network

87
00:03:12.900 --> 00:03:14.790
on your VPC.

88
00:03:14.790 --> 00:03:17.160
Or an ENI, or whatever you want.

89
00:03:17.160 --> 00:03:18.990
You can also use VPC Flow Logs to send them

90
00:03:18.990 --> 00:03:20.550
again into CloudWatch Logs.

91
00:03:20.550 --> 00:03:23.460
Here we can set up a metric filter to look, for example

92
00:03:23.460 --> 00:03:26.460
for the SSH or the RDP protocols.

93
00:03:26.460 --> 00:03:28.916
And if we realize that there is a lot more SSH

94
00:03:28.916 --> 00:03:31.740
or RDP than usual, then trigger a CloudWatch alarm

95
00:03:31.740 --> 00:03:34.710
and send an alert into an Amazon SNS topic,

96
00:03:34.710 --> 00:03:37.950
because something fishy may be happening on your network.

97
00:03:37.950 --> 00:03:40.230
Or we can use a VPC Flow Log,

98
00:03:40.230 --> 00:03:43.890
and then we send everything into an S3 bucket for storage,

99
00:03:43.890 --> 00:03:45.360
and we use Amazon Athena

100
00:03:45.360 --> 00:03:48.420
to analyze the VPC Flow Logs with SQL.

101
00:03:48.420 --> 00:03:51.840
And we can even visualize that with Amazon QuickSight.

102
00:03:51.840 --> 00:03:54.480
So there is one case where you have VPC Flow Logs

103
00:03:54.480 --> 00:03:55.710
with a NAT gateway,

104
00:03:55.710 --> 00:03:58.230
and it turns out that you have this use case

105
00:03:58.230 --> 00:03:59.280
and it seems weird to you.

106
00:03:59.280 --> 00:04:03.270
So the VPC Flow Logs show Action = ACCEPT

107
00:04:03.270 --> 00:04:06.450
for incoming traffic from public IP addresses

108
00:04:06.450 --> 00:04:08.040
onto your VPC.

109
00:04:08.040 --> 00:04:11.490
But you know that NAT gateways don't accept traffic

110
00:04:11.490 --> 00:04:12.600
from the internet.

111
00:04:12.600 --> 00:04:14.670
So you're wondering if your NAT gateway

112
00:04:14.670 --> 00:04:17.490
is actually accepting inbound traffic from the internet

113
00:04:17.490 --> 00:04:20.280
because you still see it in your VPC Flow Log.

114
00:04:20.280 --> 00:04:24.150
So this is the architecture where the internet goes through

115
00:04:24.150 --> 00:04:25.380
and talks to your NAT gateway

116
00:04:25.380 --> 00:04:27.330
and maybe to your private subnets,

117
00:04:27.330 --> 00:04:29.190
so you wanna verify this.

118
00:04:29.190 --> 00:04:33.240
So in that case, that is possible if the inbound traffic

119
00:04:33.240 --> 00:04:35.610
is permitted by the security group

120
00:04:35.610 --> 00:04:38.790
or the NACLs of your NAT gateway itself.

121
00:04:38.790 --> 00:04:41.730
But when it arrives to your NAT gateway,

122
00:04:41.730 --> 00:04:42.810
it's going to be dropped

123
00:04:42.810 --> 00:04:46.500
because the NAT gateway itself doesn't allow traffic.

124
00:04:46.500 --> 00:04:48.570
So to confirm this behavior,

125
00:04:48.570 --> 00:04:50.310
you run the following query

126
00:04:50.310 --> 00:04:52.890
in your CloudWatch Log Group's insights.

127
00:04:52.890 --> 00:04:56.310
You do a filter, and in the filter you look

128
00:04:56.310 --> 00:05:00.990
for the destination address to be xxx.xxx

129
00:05:00.990 --> 00:05:04.860
where this is the first two octets of your VPC CIDR.

130
00:05:04.860 --> 00:05:08.700
So you look at destination being within your VPC,

131
00:05:08.700 --> 00:05:12.857
and your source address to be like the public IP

132
00:05:12.857 --> 00:05:14.700
that's trying to reach you.

133
00:05:14.700 --> 00:05:16.740
So you're going to analyze whether or not

134
00:05:16.740 --> 00:05:19.080
you see any traffic from public IP

135
00:05:19.080 --> 00:05:22.260
that's trying to reach you onto your VPC.

136
00:05:22.260 --> 00:05:25.830
And then you sum the bytes as the byte transferred

137
00:05:25.830 --> 00:05:28.470
by source address and destination address.

138
00:05:28.470 --> 00:05:31.440
And this is going to view a list of all the traffic

139
00:05:31.440 --> 00:05:35.040
between the public IP and your VPC.

140
00:05:35.040 --> 00:05:38.880
And it turns out that you will see the traffic

141
00:05:38.880 --> 00:05:41.520
on the private IP of the NAT gateway

142
00:05:41.520 --> 00:05:43.350
because it is accepting traffic

143
00:05:43.350 --> 00:05:47.700
because of the security group or the network ACL.

144
00:05:47.700 --> 00:05:51.330
But then you will not see any other line than this one,

145
00:05:51.330 --> 00:05:53.580
because the unsolicited traffic

146
00:05:53.580 --> 00:05:56.850
onto your NAT gateway has then been dropped.

147
00:05:56.850 --> 00:05:59.610
So that's it for this lecture, I hope you liked it,

148
00:05:59.610 --> 00:06:01.560
and I will see you in the next lecture.

